## 1. Why Should We Use Angular?

Angular is one of the most popular JavaScript frameworks for building web applications, made and maintained by **Google**. Many developers use it — the reasons come down to the improvements it gives us, covered below.

---

## 2. Performance

- **Faster initial loads** — the app starts quickly.
- **Efficient change detection** — the screen updates efficiently.
- **Improved rendering time** — updates render fast.
- **Better modularity** — code is split into clean, separate parts.
- **Dependency injection** — a tidy way to share code and services.
- **Testability** — the app is easy to test.

> Don't worry if these terms feel unfamiliar — each will be covered in detail in later classes.

Angular keeps getting faster with every release. It's currently on **version 22**, with each version focused on smaller, faster apps. Speed is a deliberate design goal, not an accident.

---

## 3. Mobile Support

Angular is built with mobile in mind from the ground up — touch interfaces, small screens, and limited hardware are all considered in its design.

This means you can build **one single application** that works across both mobile and desktop, without bolting on separate third-party tools.

---

## 4. Language Choices

Angular supports multiple languages to write your app in:

- Plain JavaScript
- Newer versions of the JavaScript standard
- **TypeScript** (most popular, and what this course uses)

Angular itself is built using TypeScript. This raises two natural questions, answered next: *What is ECMAScript?* and *What is TypeScript?*

---

## 5. What is ECMAScript?

ECMAScript is the **official name for the JavaScript language standard**. Many versions have been released over the years, each adding new features.

- **ES6 = ES2015** — these are the same thing, just renamed. It introduced classes, modules, and arrow functions.
- A new version has released roughly every year since, and modern browsers support these features directly.

**In one line:** ECMAScript is just the official name for the JavaScript language, updated yearly with new features.

---

## 6. What is TypeScript?

TypeScript is a **free, open-source language developed by Microsoft**. It's a **superset of JavaScript** — meaning:

- Every bit of valid JavaScript is already valid TypeScript.
- TypeScript adds more on top.
- A **compiling** step turns TypeScript into plain JavaScript that browsers can run.

**What it adds:**
- **Types**
- Object-oriented features (classes, interfaces, inheritance) — familiar if you've used Java or C++

This makes writing, maintaining, and refactoring Angular apps genuinely pleasant, which is why TypeScript is the #1 choice for Angular developers.

**Do I need to learn TypeScript first?** No — every concept needed will be covered step by step during the course. It's also fully supported in all popular code editors.

